From: François Guerraz <kubrick@fgv6.net>
Subject: [PATCH] netfilter: flowtable: match the PPPoE session in hardware
 offload rules

A flow received in a PPPoE session is looked up in software by its
session: nf_flow_tuple_encap() records the session ID of a PPPoE frame
in the tuple, the flow's own tuple carries the session it was set up
with (nft_dev_path_info(), flow_offload_fill_route()), and the encap
IDs are part of the lookup key. A frame of another session, or a plain
IP frame with the same 5-tuple, does not hit the flow.

nf_flow_rule_match() only turns 802.1Q encapsulations into match keys.
For a direction of a flow received through a PPPoE interface, the
hardware rule therefore matches the ingress device and the inner
5-tuple (the VLAN key is filled but not flagged in used_keys), and
nothing says that the packets arrive in a PPPoE session, let alone which
one. A driver that programs the rule as given accepts frames that
software never forwards through this flow: plain IP, or another session,
on the same port with the same 5-tuple.

Add the session to the match as FLOW_DISSECTOR_KEY_PPPOE, filled the way
cls_flower fills it: the session ID, the PPP protocol of the tuple's
family (PPP_IP or PPP_IPV6) and the type ETH_P_PPP_SES, all with full
masks, and basic.n_proto keeps the inner protocol as before. Set the key
in used_keys so that drivers can program it or decline the rule. PPPoE
is always the innermost encapsulation of the tuple, after any VLAN.

The key is set on whichever direction of a flow is received through a
PPPoE interface: the reply of a connection routed out through it, or the
original direction of one opened from that side (a DNAT port forward,
inbound IPv6). act_ct flows have no encapsulation. Of the drivers that
handle TC_SETUP_FT, mtk_eth_soc and airoha read the keys they know and
ignore this one, as they already ignore the VLAN; mlx5 checks used_keys
against the keys it supports and now declines such a rule, as it already
declines the opposite direction, which carries FLOW_ACTION_PPPOE_PUSH.

The VLAN keys set by this function are still not in used_keys; that is
left alone here.

Assisted-by: Claude:claude-opus-5-5
---
 include/net/netfilter/nf_flow_table.h |  1 +
 net/netfilter/nf_flow_table_offload.c | 31 +++++++++++++++++++++++++++
 2 files changed, 32 insertions(+)

--- a/include/net/netfilter/nf_flow_table.h
+++ b/include/net/netfilter/nf_flow_table.h
@@ -25,6 +25,7 @@ struct nf_flow_key {
 	struct flow_dissector_key_basic			basic;
 	struct flow_dissector_key_vlan			vlan;
 	struct flow_dissector_key_vlan			cvlan;
+	struct flow_dissector_key_pppoe			pppoe;
 	union {
 		struct flow_dissector_key_ipv4_addrs	ipv4;
 		struct flow_dissector_key_ipv6_addrs	ipv6;
--- a/net/netfilter/nf_flow_table_offload.c
+++ b/net/netfilter/nf_flow_table_offload.c
@@ -89,6 +89,35 @@ static void nf_flow_rule_vlan_match(struct flow_dissector_key_vlan *key,
 	mask->vlan_tpid = 0xffff;
 }
 
+/* Match the PPPoE session a flow is received in, as the software path
+ * does: nf_flow_tuple_encap() records the session ID of a PPPoE frame in
+ * the tuple it looks the flow up with. The key is filled the way
+ * cls_flower fills it, with the PPP protocol of the tuple's family.
+ */
+static void nf_flow_rule_pppoe_match(struct nf_flow_match *match,
+				     const struct flow_offload_tuple *tuple)
+{
+	struct nf_flow_key *mask = &match->mask;
+	struct nf_flow_key *key = &match->key;
+	int i;
+
+	for (i = 0; i < tuple->encap_num; i++) {
+		if (tuple->encap[i].proto != htons(ETH_P_PPP_SES))
+			continue;
+
+		NF_FLOW_DISSECTOR(match, FLOW_DISSECTOR_KEY_PPPOE, pppoe);
+		key->pppoe.session_id = htons(tuple->encap[i].id);
+		mask->pppoe.session_id = htons(0xffff);
+		key->pppoe.ppp_proto = htons(tuple->l3proto == AF_INET ?
+					     PPP_IP : PPP_IPV6);
+		mask->pppoe.ppp_proto = htons(0xffff);
+		key->pppoe.type = htons(ETH_P_PPP_SES);
+		mask->pppoe.type = htons(0xffff);
+		match->dissector.used_keys |= BIT_ULL(FLOW_DISSECTOR_KEY_PPPOE);
+		break;
+	}
+}
+
 static int nf_flow_rule_match(struct nf_flow_match *match,
 			      const struct flow_offload_tuple *tuple,
 			      struct dst_entry *other_dst)
@@ -168,6 +197,8 @@ static int nf_flow_rule_match(struct nf_flow_match *match,
 	match->dissector.used_keys |= BIT_ULL(key->control.addr_type);
 	mask->basic.n_proto = 0xffff;
 
+	nf_flow_rule_pppoe_match(match, tuple);
+
 	switch (tuple->l4proto) {
 	case IPPROTO_TCP:
 		key->tcp.flags = 0;
